// wp_wait_durable — opt-in durability ack (AXVERITY_WATERMARK_ACK_V1,
// adapted to this repo's own architecture, no cross-repo reuse). Blocks
// the calling context until block `target`'s bytes have been durably
// been made durable by the flusher — under 5d that means a slab_tick sweep
// (or the end-of-stream seal) has fsynced it, NOT merely that it was appended.
//
// Reuses ONLY existing bridge primitives (bchan_take/cell_load_raw/
// loop_while) — no new bridge code, no M1-authored wait() handler (M1
// composites can't be used as wait()'s Fn argument — only bridge-native
// fns can, verified empirically: `Value` is not a legal M1 surface type
// for a fn param/return at all, parse error at the fn's own token, and
// graphcore's own only wait() call site — gcore_flush_step.m1 — passes a
// bridge-resident fn, never an M1 one). `cell` is the durable-watermark
// cell handle flusher minted at its own startup (see src/flusher.m1) and
// published on wp-durable-cell-pub; `target` is the block sequence
// number to wait for (the same `seq` flusher's own footer-chain already
// uses as the unambiguous logical identity — never a raw pointer, since
// pool blocks get physically reused across many seqs, per
// wp_mem_controller_step.m1's own header note).
//
// State is Text, "<cell> FS <target>" (chr(28), the same field-separator
// convention graphcore's own loop_while state encoding uses throughout,
// e.g. gr_qmore/gr_join) — not a Value/list, for the same reason: Value
// is not an accepted M1 type annotation, only a registry contract type.
//
// No explicit subscribe-before-check step is needed the way an
// event_subscribe/wait design would require: bchan_take blocks on the
// SAME durable-advance queue flusher bchan_sends to, and loop_while's
// condition (wp_wait_durable_more) is checked BEFORE every bchan_take,
// against the watermark cell itself (the actual source of truth) — so a
// target that's already durable by the time this is called never touches
// the channel at all, and a notification meant for a later target is
// never "stolen": consuming any notification only ever causes an extra
// harmless recheck, since the watermark is monotonic and shared.
// ⚠ AXVERITY_EXTENT_WRITE_PATH step 5a — THIS RETURNS FOR EVERY TARGET ONCE
// THE RUN ENDS, INCLUDING ONES THAT WERE NEVER WRITTEN.
//
// wp_flusher_eos bumps the durable watermark to 1000000000 at end of stream so
// that a caller parked on a seq the run will never reach is released instead
// of blocking forever. A notification alone cannot do it — the notification is
// only a hint and the recheck below would still fail — so the cell itself has
// to move.
//
// The consequence is a contract on YOU, not on this fn: a return means EITHER
// `target` became durable OR the run ended. If the difference matters, re-read
// the run's final seq from wp-ack-final-cell AFTER this returns and discard the
// case where `target` is past it. wp_ack_bench_step is the worked example.
//
// This is deliberately stated here rather than only at that call site, because
// the failure mode is silent: a consumer that skips the recheck treats "the
// run is over" as "your block is durable" and gets no error at all.
fn wp_wait_durable(cell: Int, target: Int) -> Unit {
  let fs   = chr(Int(28))
  let init = str_concat(int_to_str(cell), str_concat(fs, int_to_str(target)))
  let _fin = loop_while(init, wp_wait_durable_more, wp_wait_durable_step, Int(100000000))
  Unit()
}
